TYPE 2 — MONOTONIC STACK
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ "For each element, find the next/previous greater/smaller element"
→ "Largest rectangle", "trapping rain water"
→ Any problem where you need the nearest element satisfying a condition

The idea: maintain a stack that is always sorted (monotonically).
When a new element violates the order → pop until order is restored.
Each popped element found its "answer" — the current element triggered it.

DECREASING STACK → finds NEXT GREATER element
(keep stack decreasing; pop when current > top)

INCREASING STACK → finds NEXT SMALLER element
(keep stack increasing; pop when current < top)

TEMPLATE — Next Greater Element:

vector<int> result(n, -1);
stack<int> st;   // stores indices

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    while (!st.empty() && nums[st.top()] < nums[i]) {
        result[st.top()] = nums[i];   // nums[i] is the next greater
        st.pop();
    }
    st.push(i);
}
EXAMPLE — Daily Temperatures
https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-temperatures

For each day, find the number of days until a warmer temperature.
Decreasing stack of indices. When temps[i] > temps[top] → pop.
Answer for the popped index = i - popped_index.

vector<int> dailyTemperatures(vector<int>& temps) {
    vector<int> ans(temps.size(), 0);
    stack<int> st;
    for (int i = 0; i < temps.size(); i++) {
        while (!st.empty() && temps[i] > temps[st.top()]) {
            ans[st.top()] = i - st.top();
            st.pop();
        }
        st.push(i);
    }
    return ans;
}
EXAMPLE — Next Greater Element II (circular array)
https://leetcode.com/problems/next-greater-element-ii

Simulate circular traversal by running the loop twice (i % n).
Only push indices in the first pass (i < n).

for (int i = 0; i < 2*n; i++) {
    while (!st.empty() && nums[st.top()] < nums[i % n]) {
        res[st.top()] = nums[i % n];
        st.pop();
    }
    if (i < n) st.push(i);
}
EXAMPLE — Largest Rectangle in Histogram
https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-rectangle-in-histogram

For each bar, find the leftmost and rightmost extent where it is the shortest.
Increasing stack. When heights[i] < heights[top] → the top bar can't extend right.
Width = i - stack_below_top - 1.

int largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {
    heights.push_back(0);   // sentinel to flush the stack at the end
    stack<int> st;
    int ans = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < heights.size(); i++) {
        while (!st.empty() && heights[i] < heights[st.top()]) {
            int h = heights[st.top()]; st.pop();
            int w = st.empty() ? i : i - st.top() - 1;
            ans = max(ans, h * w);
        }
        st.push(i);
    }
    return ans;
}
EXAMPLE — Trapping Rain Water
https://leetcode.com/problems/trapping-rain-water

Stack-based approach: find valleys between taller bars.
When current bar is taller than top → water can be trapped.
Water = (min(current, left_wall) - bottom) * width.

int trap(vector<int>& height) {
    stack<int> st;
    int ans = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < height.size(); i++) {
        while (!st.empty() && height[i] > height[st.top()]) {
            int bottom = height[st.top()]; st.pop();
            if (st.empty()) break;
            int left = st.top();
            int h = min(height[left], height[i]) - bottom;
            int w = i - left - 1;
            ans += h * w;
        }
        st.push(i);
    }
    return ans;
}
