COMMON MISTAKES AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

MISTAKE 1 — Not checking if the stack is empty before top()
Calling st.top() on an empty stack is undefined behavior.
Always check !st.empty() before accessing top.

MISTAKE 2 — Choosing the wrong monotonic direction
Decreasing stack → finds next GREATER (you pop when current is bigger).
Increasing stack → finds next SMALLER (you pop when current is smaller).
Mixing these up gives the opposite answer silently.

MISTAKE 3 — Not using indices in the monotonic stack
When you need the distance or width (Daily Temperatures, Histogram),
push indices, not values. Retrieve the value as nums[st.top()].

MISTAKE 4 — Forgetting the sentinel in histogram problems
Without appending 0 to heights, bars at the right end never get popped.
Always push a sentinel (0 for max problems, INT_MAX for min problems)
to flush the remaining stack at the end.

MISTAKE 5 — Wrong order of pops in RPN
The second pop is the LEFT operand, first pop is the RIGHT operand.
b = st.top(); st.pop(); → right operand
a = st.top(); st.pop(); → left operand
Result: a op b (not b op a). Order matters for - and /.

